  "issue_title": "[bug] _rel_frobenius_streamed under-budgets float64 temporaries (~4x): compare can still host-OOM at out-of-core n",
  "issue_body": "## Summary\r\n\r\n`_rel_frobenius_streamed` (used by `strategy --compare`) sizes each float64 row-block to `block_bytes` (default 256 MiB), but each iteration concurrently materializes **multiple** float64 buffers of that size:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nce = np.asarray(Ce[r0:r1], dtype=np.float64)   # ~block_bytes\r\ncs = np.asarray(Cs[r0:r1], dtype=np.float64)   # ~block_bytes\r\nnum_sq += float(np.sum((cs - ce) ** 2))        # subtraction + square temps\r\nden_sq += float(np.sum(ce ** 2))               # another temp\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo a \"256 MiB\" stream can peak near ~1 GiB of host RAM (plus memmap page cache), defeating the out-of-core guarantee that #166's streaming was meant to provide.\r\n\r\n## Why it matters\r\n\r\n`--compare` on disk-backed products is exactly the path that already can't hold full `n\u00d7n` float64 casts. Under-budgeting the streamed error step reintroduces host-OOM on the sizes the engine targets.\r\n\r\n## Suggested fix\r\n\r\nCharge `block_bytes` against all simultaneously live float64 row-blocks, and compute the numerator/denominator with a reused scratch (or dots) so peak residency matches the budget.\r\n\r\nOpen zone: `strategy/` only. Distinct from #166 (introduced streaming) and #287 (RAM-vs-disk matrix count).\r\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-17T13:07:31.000Z",
